/* Valuation module: lessons, cards and lesson quizzes. Questions follow the Red Book (1st ed.) order; answers are IBZY study notes. */
window.IBZY_CONTENT["valuation"] = {"units": {"val-u1": [{"id": "val-u1-l1", "title": "Enterprise value, equity value & net debt", "pages": "45–46", "cards": [{"id": "val-u1-1", "q": "Could you explain the concept of present value and how it relates to company valuations?", "a": "<p>Present value rests on the time value of money: a dollar today is worth more than a dollar in the future, because money in hand today can be invested and earn a return.</p><div class=\"formula\">PV = Σ Cash flow<sub>t</sub> ÷ (1 + r)<sup>t</sup></div><p>Intrinsic valuation applies this directly — a company is worth the sum of the present values of all the cash flows it will generate. So a highly valued company is one expected to keep earning strong returns on its invested capital (consistently investing in positive-NPV projects) with relatively low risk attached to those cash flows.</p>", "p": 45}, {"id": "val-u1-2", "q": "What is equity value and how is it calculated?", "a": "<p>Equity value — often used interchangeably with market capitalisation — is what the company is worth to its common shareholders.</p><div class=\"formula\">Equity value = Latest closing share price × Total diluted shares outstanding</div>", "p": 45}, {"id": "val-u1-3", "q": "How do you calculate the fully diluted number of shares outstanding?", "a": "<p>Use the <b>treasury stock method (TSM)</b>, which counts only dilutive securities that are in-the-money (worth exercising).</p><ol><li>Add the in-the-money options and warrants to the basic shares outstanding.</li><li>Assume the cash the company receives when those options are exercised is used to buy back shares at the current share price.</li><li>The net new shares (shares issued minus shares repurchased) are added to the basic count — that’s the fully diluted share count.</li></ol>", "p": 45}, {"id": "val-u1-4", "q": "What is enterprise value and how do you calculate it?", "a": "<p>Enterprise value (EV) is the value of the company’s <b>operations</b> to all capital providers — common shareholders, preferred shareholders and lenders. That makes it capital-structure neutral, unlike equity value, which moves with financing decisions.</p><div class=\"formula\">EV = Equity value + Net debt + Preferred stock + Minority interest</div>", "p": 45}, {"id": "val-u1-5", "q": "How do you calculate equity value from enterprise value?", "a": "<p>Work backwards: subtract the claims of everyone other than common shareholders. Net debt is gross debt and debt-like claims, net of cash and non-operating assets.</p><div class=\"formula\">Equity value = EV − Net debt − Preferred stock − Minority interest</div><p>Equity value is just the residual belonging to equity holders; EV is the value available to every provider of capital.</p>", "p": 45}, {"id": "val-u1-6", "q": "Which line items are included in the calculation of net debt?", "a": "<p>Start with all interest-bearing debt — short- and long-term loans and bonds — plus non-equity financial claims such as preferred stock and non-controlling interests. Then subtract cash and other non-operating assets, such as short-term investments and equity investments.</p><div class=\"formula\">Net debt = Total debt − Cash &amp; equivalents</div>", "p": 46}, {"id": "val-u1-7", "q": "When calculating enterprise value, why do we add net debt?", "a": "<p>The logic of netting is that the cash on the balance sheet could be used to pay down debt if needed. So cash is offset against debt, and the net amount is what a buyer of the operations is effectively taking on. That’s also why many leverage ratios use net debt instead of gross debt.</p>", "p": 46}, {"id": "val-u1-8", "q": "What is the difference between enterprise value and equity value?", "a": "<p>EV reflects <b>all</b> stakeholders — equity holders, lenders and preferred holders — so it’s independent of capital structure and closer to the value of the business itself. Equity value only reflects common shareholders.</p><p>A good illustration: at one point Zoom’s market cap was higher than seven of the biggest airlines combined, which shocked investors. What that comparison missed:</p><ul><li>the airlines’ equity values were temporarily depressed by travel restrictions, before the government bailout was announced;</li><li>the airlines are mature businesses with far more debt — much more of their value belongs to non-equity stakeholders, which equity value ignores.</li></ul>", "p": 46}, {"id": "val-u1-9", "q": "Could a company have a negative net debt balance and have an enterprise value lower than its equity value?", "a": "<p>Yes. Negative net debt just means the company has more cash than debt — Apple and Microsoft are classic examples, sitting on huge cash piles. For companies like these, EV is <b>lower</b> than equity value.</p><p>It makes sense once you remember EV values only the operations and excludes non-operating assets. Cash is treated as non-operating, so a cash-rich company’s equity value (which includes the cash) will exceed its EV.</p>", "p": 46}, {"id": "val-u1-10", "q": "Can the enterprise value of a company turn negative?", "a": "<p>Yes, though it’s rare. A negative EV means the company’s net cash (cash minus debt) is larger than its entire equity value — the market is valuing the operations at less than nothing.</p>", "p": 46}]}, {"id": "val-u1-l2", "title": "EV adjustments & the main valuation approaches", "pages": "46–49", "cards": [{"id": "val-u1-11", "q": "If a company raises $250 million in additional debt, how would its enterprise value change?", "a": "<p>In theory, <b>not at all</b> — EV is capital-structure neutral. Debt goes up $250m, but so does cash, so net debt is unchanged.</p><p>In practice, the cost of the financing (fees and interest expense) reduces profitability, which could pull the valuation down somewhat.</p>", "p": 46}, {"id": "val-u1-12", "q": "Why do we add minority interest to equity value in the calculation of enterprise value?", "a": "<p>Minority interest is the part of a subsidiary the parent <i>doesn’t</i> own. Under US GAAP, once a parent owns more than 50% (but under 100%) of a subsidiary, it consolidates 100% of the subsidiary’s financials.</p><p>So EV-based multiples have the full consolidated metric (e.g. 100% of EBITDA) in the denominator. To keep numerator and denominator consistent, minority interest has to be added to EV — otherwise you’d be comparing a partial value with a 100% earnings figure.</p>", "p": 47}, {"id": "val-u1-13", "q": "How are convertible bonds and preferred equity with a convertible feature accounted for when calculating enterprise value?", "a": "<p>It depends whether they’re in- or out-of-the-money at the valuation date:</p><ul><li><b>In-the-money</b> (share price above the conversion price): treat them like extra equity dilution — add the converted shares to the diluted share count.</li><li><b>Out-of-the-money:</b> treat them as a financial liability, like debt.</li></ul>", "p": 47}, {"id": "val-u1-14", "q": "What are the two main approaches to valuation?", "a": "<p><b>Intrinsic valuation:</b> value comes from the business’s own ability to generate cash flow. The DCF is the most common form — the business is worth the present value of its future free cash flows.</p><p><b>Relative valuation:</b> value comes from how similar companies are priced. You apply the average or median multiple of a peer group (often EV/EBITDA or P/E) to the target. Using current market prices of public peers is <b>trading comps</b>; using prices paid for recently acquired peers is <b>transaction comps</b>.</p>", "p": 47}, {"id": "val-u1-15", "q": "What are the most common valuation methods used in finance?", "a": "<table><tr><th>Method</th><th>How it values the company</th></tr><tr><td>Comparable company analysis (trading comps)</td><td>How the market currently values similar public companies.</td></tr><tr><td>Comparable transactions (transaction comps)</td><td>What buyers paid to acquire similar companies in recent years.</td></tr><tr><td>Discounted cash flow (DCF)</td><td>Projected cash flows discounted at a rate reflecting their risk.</td></tr><tr><td>Leveraged buyout (LBO) analysis</td><td>The maximum price a financial buyer could pay under a highly leveraged structure and still hit its target return.</td></tr><tr><td>Liquidation analysis</td><td>For distressed companies — the value of the assets in a hypothetical worst-case liquidation.</td></tr></table>", "p": 47}, {"id": "val-u1-16", "q": "Among the DCF, comparable companies analysis, and transaction comps, which approach yields the highest valuation?", "a": "<p>Usually <b>transaction comps</b>. Those multiples come from completed acquisitions, so they include a <b>control premium</b> — often 25–50% above the pre-deal market price. That pushes them above trading comps or a standalone DCF.</p>", "p": 48}, {"id": "val-u1-17", "q": "Which of the valuation methodologies is the most variable in terms of output?", "a": "<p>The <b>DCF</b>. It depends on forward-looking projections and discretionary assumptions, so small changes swing the answer a lot. Trading and transaction comps are anchored to prices actually paid for similar companies; there’s still judgement involved, but the outputs vary less.</p>", "p": 48}, {"id": "val-u1-18", "q": "Contrast the discounted cash flow (DCF) approach to the trading comps approach.", "a": "<table><tr><th></th><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr><tr><td><b>DCF</b></td><td>Values the company on its own forecast cash flows — seen as the most direct and academically rigorous measure of value. Based on fundamentals, independent of the market.</td><td>Very sensitive to assumptions. Forecasting gets harder the further out you go. The use of beta in WACC is widely criticised, and the terminal value is often ~¾ of the total value.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Trading comps</b></td><td>Based on how the market actually prices similar businesses — many see it as a more realistic view of what the company would trade at.</td><td>The market isn’t always right, so it’s <i>pricing</i>, not fundamental valuation. Few companies are truly comparable (“apples and oranges”). And comps make just as many assumptions as a DCF — they’re just implicit rather than explicit.</td></tr></table>", "p": 48}, {"id": "val-u1-19", "q": "How can you determine which valuation method to use?", "a": "<p>Every method has weaknesses, so use several and arrive at a <b>range</b>. Multiple methods give a more defensible estimate and let each one sanity-check the others.</p><p>DCF and comps are often used together. When valuing an acquisition target, an analyst would look at premiums paid in comparable transactions to see what the buyer should realistically expect to pay, and also run a DCF to show how far market prices sit from intrinsic value.</p><p>Investors do the same: an opportunity may exist where comps-based market values are well below DCF-based values — keeping in mind how sensitive the DCF is to its assumptions.</p>", "p": 48}, {"id": "val-u1-20", "q": "Would you agree with the statement that relative valuation relies less on the discretionary assumptions of individuals?", "a": "<p>Not really. Comps and a DCF give different answers only because their assumptions differ — if the comps’ implied assumptions matched the DCF’s explicit ones, the two would give the same value.</p><p>Applying a peer multiple still implicitly assumes things about future cash flows, cost of capital and returns — the same things a DCF makes you spell out. The difference is that you’re borrowing other investors’ assumptions. Relative valuation assumes the market consensus is roughly right and that investors are rational.</p>", "p": 49}]}, {"id": "val-u1-l3", "title": "Free cash flow, capital structure & buybacks", "pages": "49–51", "cards": [{"id": "val-u1-21", "q": "What does free cash flow (FCF) represent?", "a": "<p>FCF is a company’s <b>discretionary</b> cash flow — what’s left after the recurring spending needed to keep operating.</p><div class=\"formula\">FCF = Cash from operations − Capex</div><p>The rest of investing (other than capex) and all of financing are left out, because those are optional decisions management makes, not requirements of running the business.</p>", "p": 49}, {"id": "val-u1-22", "q": "Why are periodic acquisitions excluded from the calculation of FCF?", "a": "<p>FCF should only capture cash flows from the core, recurring operations. Capex is recurring and necessary — the business can’t keep running without it. An acquisition is a one-off, unpredictable event, so it doesn’t belong in a measure of ongoing cash generation.</p>", "p": 49}, {"id": "val-u1-23", "q": "Explain the importance of excluding non-operating income/(expenses) for valuations.", "a": "<p>Whether you’re running a DCF or comps, you’re trying to value the <b>operations</b>, so the numbers need to be normalised to the core business.</p><p><b>DCF:</b> projected cash flows should come only from recurring operations — selling the company’s goods and services. Investment income, dividends received or gains on asset sales are non-recurring and come from discretionary decisions, so they’re excluded.</p><p><b>Comps:</b> you’re benchmarking the target’s core operations against peers, so to get as close to “apples to apples” as possible, strip out non-core income/expenses and non-recurring items.</p><p>Every method has some built-in bias, which is why several are used together.</p>", "p": 49}, {"id": "val-u1-24", "q": "Define free cash flow yield and compare it to dividend yield and P/E ratios.", "a": "<div class=\"formula\">FCF yield = FCF per share ÷ Current share price</div><p>(with FCF = cash from operations − capex)</p><p><b>vs. dividend yield:</b> both measure equity returns relative to the share price, but FCF yield is based on cash <i>generated</i>, not cash actually paid out. That makes it a better fundamental measure, because many companies pay no dividend or pay out an arbitrary share of their FCF.</p><p><b>vs. P/E:</b> flip FCF yield and you get price ÷ FCF per share — a cash-flow version of P/E. The upside is that it benchmarks price against real cash instead of accrual profit; the downside is that cash flow is volatile, and swings in working capital or deferred revenue can distort it.</p>", "p": 50}, {"id": "val-u1-25", "q": "Could you define what the capital structure of a company represents?", "a": "<p>Capital structure is how a company funds its operations and growth — usually a mix of debt and equity, each with its own pros and cons.</p><p>As companies mature and build a track record of profits, their default risk falls, so they can borrow more easily and more cheaply. That’s why leverage tends to rise as a company matures. The optimal capital structure is the debt/equity mix that minimises the cost of capital and so maximises firm value.</p>", "p": 50}, {"id": "val-u1-26", "q": "Why would a company issue equity vs. debt (and vice versa)?", "a": "<table><tr><th></th><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr><tr><td><b>Equity</b></td><td>No required payments, so more flexibility. Dividends are discretionary in timing and size. Access to a large investor base and network.</td><td>Dilutes ownership and has a high cost of capital. Public equity brings regulation, scrutiny from shareholders and analysts, and full disclosure. Management can be voted out if the company underperforms.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Debt</b></td><td>Interest is tax-deductible (though recent reform caps this for highly levered companies). No dilution and a lower cost of capital. Leverage enforces discipline on management.</td><td>Required interest and principal create default risk. Covenants restrict activities like more borrowing, dividends or acquisitions. Less room for error — bad decisions hurt more.</td></tr></table>", "p": 50}, {"id": "val-u1-27", "q": "What are share buybacks and under which circumstances would they be most appropriate?", "a": "<p>A buyback is when a company uses cash to repurchase its own shares — either through a tender offer to shareholders or on the open market. It shows up as a cash outflow on the CFS and as treasury stock on the balance sheet.</p><p>Ideally it’s done when management thinks the market is <b>undervaluing</b> the shares. Fewer shares in circulation immediately raises EPS (and possibly the P/E), and the market may read it as a signal that management is confident about future earnings.</p>", "p": 51}, {"id": "val-u1-28", "q": "Why would a company repurchase shares? What would the impact on the share price and financial statements be?", "a": "<p>Mainly to move cash from the balance sheet to shareholders — like a dividend, except instead of every holder getting cash, some holders are bought out.</p><p><b>Share price:</b> theoretically neutral. The share count falls, but equity value falls too because the company has less cash, so price per share shouldn’t change if shares are fairly priced. In reality, it depends on the signal:</p><ul><li>A cash-rich but risky company whose cash investors discount may see its price <b>rise</b> — the smaller share count outweighs the lower equity value.</li><li>If investors read it as “we have nothing better to invest in”, lower perceived growth can more than offset the smaller denominator, and the price <b>falls</b>.</li></ul><p><b>Financials</b> for a $100m buyback: cash down $100m (credit), treasury stock up $100m (debit), reducing equity.</p>", "p": 51}, {"id": "val-u1-29", "q": "Why might a company prefer to repurchase shares over the issuance of a dividend?", "a": "<ul><li>Dividends are “double taxed” — the income is taxed at the corporate level (dividends aren’t deductible) and again for the shareholder.</li><li>Buybacks reduce the share count, which lifts EPS and can support the share price.</li><li>They offset dilution from the stock-based compensation many companies use to conserve cash.</li><li>They signal management thinks the shares are undervalued.</li><li>They can be one-off. Dividends are seen as a long-term commitment, and cutting one is punished hard by the market — investors assume profits are about to fall — so once started, dividends are rarely cut.</li></ul>", "p": 51}]}, {"id": "val-u1-l4", "title": "Dividends, CAGR vs IRR & special valuations", "pages": "52–53", "cards": [{"id": "val-u1-30", "q": "A company with $100 million in net income and a P/E multiple of 15x is considering raising $200 million in debt to pay out a one-time cash dividend. How would you decide if this is a good idea?", "a": "<p>Assume the P/E stays at 15x, a 5% cost of debt and (for simplicity) no tax effect:</p><ul><li>New interest = $200m × 5% = $10m, so net income falls from $100m to $90m.</li><li>Equity value falls from $1,500m (15 × $100m) to $1,350m (15 × $90m) — a $150m drop.</li><li>But shareholders receive $200m of cash immediately.</li></ul><p>Net benefit to shareholders ≈ <b>$50m</b> ($200m − $150m).</p><p>The answer depends heavily on those assumptions. A higher cost of debt could wipe out the benefit. The biggest weak spot is assuming the P/E holds at 15x — P/E depends on growth, ROE and the cost of equity. Borrowing more without any extra investment or growth raises beta and the cost of equity, which pushes P/E down.</p><p>So it looks reasonable on these numbers but could easily be a bad idea. Borrowing to pay dividends isn’t sustainable forever — eventually debt levels hurt the cost of capital and the multiple. Debt should fund value-creating investment, not just pull cash out of the business.</p>", "p": 52}, {"id": "val-u1-31", "q": "When would it be most appropriate for a company to distribute dividends?", "a": "<p>Dividend payers are usually <b>low-growth</b> companies with few profitable projects left to fund. Paying dividends signals management’s confidence in long-term profitability and attracts a different shareholder base — long-term income investors.</p>", "p": 52}, {"id": "val-u1-32", "q": "What is CAGR and how do you calculate it?", "a": "<p>The compound annual growth rate is the steady annual rate at which something would have to grow to get from its beginning value to its ending value — in effect, the annualised average growth rate.</p><div class=\"formula\">CAGR = (Ending value ÷ Beginning value)<sup>1/years</sup> − 1</div>", "p": 52}, {"id": "val-u1-33", "q": "What is the difference between CAGR and IRR?", "a": "<p>Both measure returns, but:</p><ul><li><b>CAGR</b> uses just three inputs — beginning value, ending value and number of years — and smooths everything in between.</li><li><b>IRR</b> (XIRR in Excel) handles multiple cash inflows and outflows at different times, so it captures the timing and pattern of cash flows.</li></ul><p>CAGR is usually used on historical data (e.g. past revenue growth); IRR is more often used for investment decisions.</p>", "p": 52}, {"id": "val-u1-34", "q": "How would you evaluate the buy vs. rent decision in NYC?", "a": "<p>Set up comparable assumptions first — e.g. enough capital for a down payment and a 10-year horizon.</p><p><b>Buy:</b> pay the down payment, then monthly mortgage, property tax and maintenance (partly offset by tax deductions on interest and depreciation). Assume the property is sold at the end at a price reflecting NYC’s historical real estate growth. From the initial outlay, monthly costs and the final sale proceeds, calculate an IRR.</p><p><b>Rent:</b> estimate rent on comparable properties, with escalations over 10 years. Since there’s no down payment, invest that money elsewhere — say the stock market at its long-run return (~5–7%). Calculate the IRR on that path too, then compare.</p><p>The comparison isn’t perfectly apples-to-apples: owning NYC property is riskier than holding stocks because of leverage and lower liquidity. If the two IRRs came out equal, I’d lean towards renting, since I wouldn’t be compensated for the extra risk.</p>", "p": 53}, {"id": "val-u1-35", "q": "How would you value a painting?", "a": "<p>A painting has no intrinsic value — it produces no cash flows — so it can’t be valued in the traditional sense. Its price is whatever someone will pay for it. The best approach is comparable transactions: look at what buyers have paid for similar paintings in the past.</p>", "p": 53}, {"id": "val-u1-36", "q": "When would it be appropriate to use a sum-of-the-parts approach to valuing a company?", "a": "<p>When a company has several divisions with different risk/return profiles — typically a conglomerate operating in unrelated industries.</p><p>In a sum-of-the-parts (SOTP) or break-up analysis, each division is valued separately: its own discount rate, and its own peer groups for trading and transaction comps. The division values are then added up to get the value of the whole company.</p>", "p": 53}, {"id": "val-u1-37", "q": "How does valuing a private company differ from valuing a public company?", "a": "<p>The big difference is <b>data</b>. Private companies don’t have to publish their financials, and when you do get them, they’re often less complete, less standardised and less reliable — but the process is otherwise similar.</p><p>Private companies are also less liquid, so their value should be reduced by an <b>illiquidity discount</b>, usually around 10–30%.</p>", "p": 53}, {"id": "val-u1-38", "q": "What is an illiquidity discount?", "a": "<p>It reflects the fact that you can’t exit a private company investment quickly. Investors will pay more for an otherwise identical asset they can sell whenever they want. Public company shares carry that “liquidity premium”, so when you value a private company off public trading comps, you apply a discount to remove it.</p>", "p": 53}]}], "val-u2": [{"id": "val-u2-l1", "title": "The DCF, FCF types & cost of capital", "pages": "54–56", "cards": [{"id": "val-u2-1", "q": "Walk me through a DCF.", "a": "<p>The standard version is the <b>unlevered DCF</b>:</p><ol><li><b>Forecast unlevered free cash flow</b> — cash flow to the whole firm before the effect of leverage — explicitly for 5–10 years.</li><li><b>Calculate the terminal value</b> — the value of all cash flows after the forecast period — using either the growth-in-perpetuity approach or the exit multiple approach.</li><li><b>Discount</b> both the forecast cash flows and the terminal value back to today at the <b>WACC</b>. Their sum is enterprise value.</li><li><b>Bridge to equity value:</b> add non-operating assets like cash and investments, subtract debt, and subtract other non-equity claims such as minority interest.</li><li><b>Per share:</b> divide equity value by diluted shares outstanding at the valuation date. For a public company, compare it with the current share price.</li><li><b>Sensitise:</b> because the DCF is so assumption-driven, build sensitivity tables to show how the key assumptions move the implied share price.</li></ol>", "p": 54}, {"id": "val-u2-2", "q": "Conceptually, what does the discount rate represent?", "a": "<p>The discount rate is the return an investor expects given how risky the cash flows are — the minimum return they’d accept, based on other investments with similar risk.</p><p>A higher discount rate means the cash flows are seen as riskier, so they’re worth less today and a higher return is required (and vice versa).</p>", "p": 54}, {"id": "val-u2-3", "q": "What is the difference between unlevered FCF (FCFF) and levered FCF (FCFE)?", "a": "<p><b>Unlevered FCF (free cash flow to the firm):</b> cash from core operations after all operating costs and investment, <i>before</i> any payments to lenders. Start from EBIT (which excludes interest), tax-effect it — giving NOPAT, also called EBIAT — add back non-cash items, adjust for working capital and subtract capex.</p><div class=\"formula\">FCFF = EBIT × (1 − t) + D&amp;A − Change in NWC − Capex</div><p><b>Levered FCF (free cash flow to equity):</b> what’s left for equity holders <i>after</i> interest and debt repayments. Start from net income, add back non-cash items, adjust for working capital, subtract capex, then add new borrowing net of debt paydowns.</p><div class=\"formula\">FCFE = Cash from operations − Capex − Debt principal repayment (+ new borrowing)</div>", "p": 54}, {"id": "val-u2-4", "q": "What is the difference between the unlevered DCF and the levered DCF?", "a": "<p><b>Unlevered DCF:</b> discount unlevered FCF to get straight to <b>enterprise value</b>, then add non-operating assets (cash) and subtract financing claims (debt) to reach equity value. The discount rate is <b>WACC</b>, because those cash flows belong to both lenders and shareholders.</p><p><b>Levered DCF:</b> discount levered FCF to get straight to <b>equity value</b>. The discount rate is the <b>cost of equity</b>, because those cash flows belong only to shareholders. Add net debt if you want enterprise value.</p><p>In theory both give the same EV and equity value; in practice it’s very hard to make them match exactly.</p>", "p": 55}, {"id": "val-u2-5", "q": "What is the appropriate cost of capital when unlevered FCF (FCFF) and levered FCF (FCFE)?", "a": "<p><b>Unlevered FCF → WACC</b>, since it reflects the cost of capital to all providers.</p><p><b>Levered FCF → cost of equity</b>, since only equity holders have a claim on those cash flows.</p>", "p": 55}, {"id": "val-u2-6", "q": "What is the formula to calculate the weighted average cost of capital (WACC)?", "a": "<p>WACC is the opportunity cost of investing in the company — the blended return required by all capital providers, based on investments of similar risk.</p><div class=\"formula\">WACC = E/(D+E) × R<sub>e</sub> + D/(D+E) × R<sub>d</sub> × (1 − t)</div><ul><li>E/(D+E) and D/(D+E) = equity and debt weights — using <b>market values</b>, not book values</li><li>R<sub>e</sub> = cost of equity</li><li>R<sub>d</sub> = cost of debt, tax-affected because interest is deductible</li><li>t = tax rate</li></ul>", "p": 55}, {"id": "val-u2-7", "q": "If a company carries no debt, what is its WACC?", "a": "<p>Its WACC simply equals its <b>cost of equity</b>.</p><p>In practice, most mature companies add a moderate amount of debt once their operations stabilise, because lenders offer them cheaper financing than equity.</p>", "p": 55}, {"id": "val-u2-8", "q": "How is the cost of equity calculated?", "a": "<p>Most commonly with the <b>capital asset pricing model (CAPM)</b>, which ties a security’s expected return to its sensitivity to the overall market (usually proxied by the S&amp;P 500).</p><div class=\"formula\">Cost of equity = Risk-free rate + Beta × Equity risk premium</div>", "p": 55}, {"id": "val-u2-9", "q": "How do you determine the risk-free rate?", "a": "<p>In theory, the risk-free rate is the yield to maturity on default-free government bonds whose maturity matches the timing of each cash flow being discounted.</p><p>In practice, the longest-dated bonds are illiquid, so for US companies the current yield on the <b>10-year US Treasury</b> is the standard proxy.</p>", "p": 56}, {"id": "val-u2-10", "q": "What effect does a low-interest-rate environment have on DCF-derived valuations?", "a": "<p>All else equal, low rates <b>raise</b> DCF valuations: a lower risk-free rate means a lower discount rate, so future cash flows are worth more today.</p><p>There’s debate about whether to use a “normalised” risk-free rate instead. Aswath Damodaran argues against it — his view is that you should use today’s actual risk-free rates and risk premiums, not normalised ones, when valuing companies.</p>", "p": 56}]}, {"id": "val-u2-l2", "title": "Beta, risk & the cost of equity", "pages": "56–58", "cards": [{"id": "val-u2-11", "q": "Define the equity risk premium used in the CAPM formula.", "a": "<p>The equity risk premium (ERP) is the extra return investors demand for holding equities instead of risk-free securities. Historically it has been about <b>4–6%</b>, based on S&amp;P 500 returns over risk-free bond yields.</p><div class=\"formula\">ERP = Expected market return − Risk-free rate</div>", "p": 56}, {"id": "val-u2-12", "q": "Explain the concept of beta ( β).", "a": "<p>Beta measures a security’s <b>systematic</b> (non-diversifiable) risk relative to the market. Technically, it’s the covariance between the asset’s returns and the market’s returns, divided by the variance of the market’s returns.</p><p>A beta of 1.0 means the stock should move with the market — if the market is up 10%, so is the stock. A beta of 2.0 implies a 20% move when the market is up 10%.</p><table><tr><th>Beta</th><th>Market sensitivity</th></tr><tr><td>β = 0</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>β &lt; 1</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>β &gt; 1</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>β &lt; 0</td><td>Moves opposite to the market</td></tr></table>", "p": 56}, {"id": "val-u2-13", "q": "What is the difference between systematic risk and unsystematic risk?", "a": "<p><b>Systematic risk</b> (undiversifiable or market risk): risk built into the whole equity market rather than any one company or industry. You can’t diversify it away.</p><p><b>Unsystematic risk</b>: risk specific to a company or industry, which <i>can</i> be reduced by diversifying. The benefit is greatest when the portfolio spans different asset classes, industries and geographies.</p>", "p": 56}, {"id": "val-u2-14", "q": "Does a higher beta translate into a higher or lower valuation?", "a": "<p><b>Lower.</b> A high beta means more volatility relative to the market and more risk, so investors apply a higher discount rate to the company’s cash flows. All else equal, that reduces the valuation.</p>", "p": 57}, {"id": "val-u2-15", "q": "What type of sectors would have higher or lower betas?", "a": "<p>A useful test: <i>would people still need this product or service in a recession?</i></p><p><b>High beta (&gt;1):</b> cyclical industries that are more volatile than the market — autos, semiconductors, construction. People mostly buy cars and new homes when the economy is growing.</p><p><b>Low beta (&lt;1):</b> businesses selling everyday necessities — toiletries and personal care, for example — plus hospitals and healthcare facilities and utilities. Demand for these holds up whatever the economic climate.</p>", "p": 57}, {"id": "val-u2-16", "q": "What are the benefits of the industry beta approach?", "a": "<p>The industry beta approach takes the betas of a comparable peer group and applies them to the company being valued:</p><ol><li>Unlever each peer’s beta and take the average or median.</li><li>Relever it at the target’s own capital structure.</li></ol><p>Benefits:</p><ul><li>It strips out <b>company-specific noise</b> — one-off events that distort a single company’s measured correlation with the market. The result is a “normalised” beta. The underlying assumption is that the company’s business risk will converge with its peers over time.</li><li>It works for <b>private companies</b>, which have no observable beta — so it’s required for a private company DCF.</li></ul>", "p": 57}, {"id": "val-u2-17", "q": "What are the flaws of regression betas?", "a": "<p>A regression beta is the slope from regressing the stock’s historical returns against a market index’s returns. Its problems:</p><ul><li>Past correlation may not predict future performance.</li><li>It’s sensitive to choices like which index is used as the market proxy.</li><li>Company-specific events can distort it, so it may not reflect the true relationship with the market.</li><li><b>Constant capital structure:</b> it reflects the company’s <i>average past</i> debt/equity mix, not its current leverage. Leverage usually changes as a company matures, which makes this a weak assumption for forecasting, given how much debt affects beta.</li></ul>", "p": 57}, {"id": "val-u2-18", "q": "What is the impact of leverage on the beta of a company?", "a": "<p>With no debt, levered (equity) beta equals unlevered (asset) beta — it reflects business risk only.</p><p>Adding debt adds <b>financial risk</b>: there’s a higher chance of default, less margin for error and more volatility in a downturn. Levered beta captures both business and financial risk, so it’s always <b>equal to or greater than</b> unlevered beta, never lower. Unlevering removes the debt component and isolates business risk.</p>", "p": 58}, {"id": "val-u2-19", "q": "What is the typical relationship between beta and the amount of leverage used?", "a": "<p>Mature, low-beta companies tend to carry <b>more</b> debt. Their long record of profits and cash flow, and their non-cyclical nature, let them borrow cheaply.</p><p>High-beta companies are more reluctant to borrow, and when they do, the terms are worse. Think of it from the lender’s side: would you be comfortable lending to a company whose results swing wildly through the economic cycle?</p>", "p": 58}, {"id": "val-u2-20", "q": "What are the formulas used to unlever and relever beta?", "a": "<p>Betas measured from historical (or even projected) returns carry large estimation errors, so an <b>industry beta</b> is preferred. But peers’ betas are distorted by their different leverage, so first unlever them to isolate business risk:</p><div class=\"formula\">β<sub>unlevered</sub> = β<sub>levered</sub> ÷ [1 + (1 − t) × Debt/Equity]</div><p>Take the median or average unlevered beta, then relever it at the target’s capital structure:</p><div class=\"formula\">β<sub>levered</sub> = β<sub>unlevered</sub> × [1 + (1 − t) × Debt/Equity]</div>", "p": 58}, {"id": "val-u2-21", "q": "Is it possible for an asset to have a negative beta?", "a": "<p>Yes — gold is the usual example. It tends to move opposite to the market: when stocks rise, gold often falls, but in a correction or recession investors flee to gold as a safe haven, and the extra demand pushes its price up.</p>", "p": 58}]}, {"id": "val-u2-l3", "title": "Cost of debt, WACC & terminal value", "pages": "58–60", "cards": [{"id": "val-u2-22", "q": "How do you estimate the cost of debt?", "a": "<p>For companies with traded debt, it’s observable: the yield on debt of equivalent risk. In general, more leverage means a higher cost of debt.</p><p>If the company has no publicly traded debt, estimate it with a <b>synthetic rating</b>: infer a credit rating from its interest coverage ratio, then add the default spread for that rating to the risk-free rate.</p>", "p": 58}, {"id": "val-u2-23", "q": "Which is typically higher, the cost of debt or the cost of equity?", "a": "<p>The <b>cost of equity</b>. Interest is tax-deductible, creating a tax shield that lowers the effective cost of debt. And equity holders take more risk: they aren’t promised fixed payments and sit at the bottom of the capital structure in a liquidation, so they demand a higher return.</p>", "p": 59}, {"id": "val-u2-24", "q": "Since the cost of equity is higher than the cost of debt, why not finance using only debt?", "a": "<p>Because the cost of debt doesn’t stay low as you add more. More leverage means higher default risk, so lenders (and shareholders) demand more.</p><p>As debt rises from zero, WACC initially <b>falls</b>, thanks to the interest tax shield. It keeps falling until the optimal capital structure is reached. Beyond that point, the expected cost of financial distress outweighs the tax benefit, and WACC starts <b>rising</b> as risk increases for all stakeholders.</p><p>Debt also brings covenants that restrict what the company can do — for example, capping leverage ratios or requiring minimum coverage ratios. So most companies end up with a mix of debt and equity.</p>", "p": 59}, {"id": "val-u2-25", "q": "What is the difference between WACC and IRR?", "a": "<p><b>IRR:</b> the return a project or investment actually earns — the rate at which the initial investment grows into the ending value. Equivalently, the discount rate that makes the NPV of the cash flows equal zero.</p><p><b>WACC:</b> the <i>minimum</i> return required by all debt and equity providers.</p><p>A common decision rule: pursue a project if its IRR exceeds the WACC.</p>", "p": 59}, {"id": "val-u2-26", "q": "Which would have more of an impact on a DCF, the discount rate or sales growth rate?", "a": "<p>Both should be sensitised in a proper model, but the <b>discount rate</b> has a far bigger impact than operating assumptions like the sales growth rate.</p>", "p": 59}, {"id": "val-u2-27", "q": "How is the terminal value calculated?", "a": "<p><b>Growth in perpetuity (Gordon Growth):</b> assume cash flows grow at a constant rate forever after the forecast period, and plug that into the perpetuity formula.</p><div class=\"formula\">TV = FCF<sub>n+1</sub> ÷ (r − g)</div><p><b>Exit multiple:</b> apply a multiple — usually EV/EBITDA — to the final forecast year’s metric. The multiple should reflect what comparable companies trade at in a mature state.</p>", "p": 59}, {"id": "val-u2-28", "q": "Why is it necessary to discount the terminal value back to the present?", "a": "<p>Either way you calculate it, the terminal value is the value of all cash flows beyond the forecast period, measured <b>as of the end of the forecast period</b> — the point just before the perpetuity stage begins. It’s a future value.</p><p>A DCF measures what the company is worth <i>today</i>, so that future value has to be discounted back to the valuation date like any other future cash flow.</p>", "p": 60}, {"id": "val-u2-29", "q": "For the perpetuity approach, how do you determine the long-term growth rate?", "a": "<p>It’s the rate the company can grow at forever, so it should be modest — typically <b>1–3%</b> (occasionally up to 5%). GDP growth or the risk-free rate are common proxies.</p><p>It must reflect a steady state where growth has slowed to a sustainable level. A good gut check: “Could this company really grow at X% for the next hundred years?” If not, bring it down.</p>", "p": 60}, {"id": "val-u2-30", "q": "How can the terminal value be sanity-checked if the exit multiple approach was used?", "a": "<p>Back out the <b>implied perpetuity growth rate</b> and check it’s reasonable:</p><div class=\"formula\">Implied g = (TV × r − FCF) ÷ (TV + FCF)</div><p>It works the other way too — if you used the perpetuity method, calculate the implied exit multiple:</p><div class=\"formula\">Implied exit multiple = TV ÷ Final-year EBITDA</div>", "p": 60}, {"id": "val-u2-31", "q": "What is the argument against using the exit multiple approach in a DCF?", "a": "<p>A DCF is meant to be an intrinsic, cash-flow-based valuation independent of the market. Using an exit multiple brings relative valuation back in, so the answer partly depends on how the market prices comparables.</p><p>Even so, it’s widely used in practice because the assumption is easier to discuss and defend than a perpetuity growth rate.</p>", "p": 60}]}, {"id": "val-u2-l4", "title": "DCF mechanics, timing & when not to use a DCF", "pages": "60–62", "cards": [{"id": "val-u2-32", "q": "What is the purpose of using a mid-year convention in a DCF model?", "a": "<p>Without it, a DCF implicitly assumes every year’s cash flow arrives on the last day of the year. In reality, cash comes in steadily through the year. The mid-year convention treats each year’s cash flow as arriving at the <b>midpoint</b> of the period.</p><p>Because the cash is assumed to arrive earlier, the valuation goes <b>up</b>. For example, a Year 5 cash flow would be discounted using 4.5 years instead of 5.</p>", "p": 60}, {"id": "val-u2-33", "q": "Could you give me an example of when the mid-year convention might be inappropriate?", "a": "<p>For highly <b>seasonal</b> businesses. Many retailers earn a disproportionate share of sales in Q3 and Q4 — especially those focused on winter clothing. For a company like Canada Goose, most cash comes in late in the year, so the standard end-of-period assumption may be more accurate than mid-year.</p>", "p": 61}, {"id": "val-u2-34", "q": "How would raising additional debt impact a DCF analysis?", "a": "<p>An unlevered DCF is capital-structure neutral, so in theory enterprise value barely changes. But if the new debt materially shifts the debt/equity weights, the valuation can change: as debt’s share rises, so does the cost of debt (higher default risk), which pushes WACC up and lowers the implied value.</p>", "p": 61}, {"id": "val-u2-35", "q": "Imagine that two companies have the same total leverage ratio with identical free cash flows and profit margins. Do both companies have the same amount of default risk?", "a": "<p>Not necessarily. If one has a lot more <b>cash</b>, it’s in a safer position, because that cash can pay down debt. That’s why cash is often thought of as “negative debt”, and why Net Debt/EBITDA is watched alongside Total Debt/EBITDA.</p><p>All else equal, the company with more excess cash and lower Net Debt/EBITDA has lower bankruptcy risk and a lower cost of debt.</p>", "p": 61}, {"id": "val-u2-36", "q": "When would a DCF be an inappropriate valuation method?", "a": "<ul><li>When you don’t have the financial statements — a credible DCF is very hard to build. If all you have is a figure like revenue or EBIT, comps are much easier to apply.</li><li>When the company isn’t expected to generate positive cash flow for a long time. Most of the value is pushed into the distant future, and the DCF loses credibility.</li></ul>", "p": 61}, {"id": "val-u2-37", "q": "Why is a DCF not used to value early stage startups?", "a": "<p>It <i>can</i> be used on cash-flow-negative startups, as long as there’s a believable path to positive cash flow. But it becomes unreliable when the company may not reach stable growth for 15+ years — forecasting cash flows that far out is close to guesswork.</p><p>A DCF is most credible for mature companies with an established market position, not pre-revenue companies still working out their business model, go-to-market strategy or target customer.</p>", "p": 61}, {"id": "val-u2-38", "q": "If 80% of a DCF valuation comes from the terminal value, what should be done?", "a": "<p>Two things to check:</p><ul><li>The explicit forecast period may be too short. It should usually be 5–10 years, long enough that the company has reached normalised, stable growth by the final year.</li><li>The terminal value assumptions may be too aggressive and not reflect a genuinely stable state.</li></ul>", "p": 61}, {"id": "val-u2-39", "q": "How would you handle stock options when calculating a company’s share count?", "a": "<p>Standard practice: include <b>vested</b> (exercisable) options that are in-the-money — strike price below the current share price — and use the treasury stock method, assuming the exercise proceeds buy back shares at the current price.</p><p>Some practitioners include <i>all</i> in-the-money options, vested or not, reasoning that unvested ones will vest soon, so including them is more conservative.</p>", "p": 62}]}, {"id": "val-u2-l5", "title": "Dilution, leases, taxes & the DDM", "pages": "62–64", "cards": [{"id": "val-u2-40", "q": "How would you handle restricted stock in the share count?", "a": "<p>Some practitioners leave restricted stock out entirely because it hasn’t vested. Increasingly, though, unvested restricted stock is <b>included</b> in the diluted share count, on the logic that it will eventually vest and including it is more conservative.</p>", "p": 62}, {"id": "val-u2-41", "q": "How would you handle convertible preferred stock in the share count?", "a": "<p>Assume conversion into common stock if the current share price is above the conversion price (i.e. converting is worth more than the liquidation value).</p><p>Example: a company with a $60 share price issued 10m convertible preferred shares for $500m. Each lets the holder take $50 (the liquidation value) or convert into one common share. Since $60 &gt; $50, assume conversion — add 10m shares to the diluted count.</p><p>If you assume conversion, <b>remove the preferred stock from net debt</b> so it isn’t counted twice.</p>", "p": 62}, {"id": "val-u2-42", "q": "How would you handle convertible bonds in the share count?", "a": "<p>Same logic: assume conversion if the conversion price is below the current share price.</p><p>Example: a company with a $60 share price issued a $500m bond convertible into 10m shares (an effective conversion price of $50). Since $60 &gt; $50, assume it converts and add 10m shares.</p><p>If conversion is assumed, <b>take the convertible out of debt</b> when calculating net debt, to avoid double counting.</p>", "p": 62}, {"id": "val-u2-43", "q": "How should operating leases be treated in a DCF valuation?", "a": "<p>They should be <b>capitalised</b>. Leases usually tie the tenant into obligations and penalties much more like debt than a simple expense — which is why, from 2019, lessees have had to show lease obligations as liabilities on the balance sheet.</p><p>So when leases are significant (retailers, capital-intensive businesses), remove the rent expense from the free cash flow build and include the present value of the lease obligations in net debt instead.</p>", "p": 62}, {"id": "val-u2-44", "q": "For forecasting purposes, do you use the effective or marginal tax rate?", "a": "<p>The key point is that whatever rate you use in a DCF’s terminal value is assumed to apply <b>forever</b>.</p><p>The effective rate is usually below the marginal rate, mainly because companies defer taxes — that’s what creates DTAs and DTLs. Using the effective rate in perpetuity would assume those deferrals continue forever, which is wrong: DTAs and DTLs unwind back to zero.</p><p>Best practice: base near-term forecasts on the historical effective rate (last 3–5 years), then move the rate towards the <b>marginal</b> rate by the time you reach the terminal (stage 2) period.</p>", "p": 62}, {"id": "val-u2-45", "q": "How does a lower tax rate impact the valuation from a DCF?", "a": "<p>Several effects pull in opposite directions:</p><ul><li><b>Higher cash flow:</b> less tax paid means higher net income and free cash flow — pushes value up.</li><li><b>Higher after-tax cost of debt:</b> the interest tax shield is worth less, so the after-tax cost of debt rises.</li><li><b>Higher levered beta:</b> with a lower tax rate, relevering produces a higher beta, raising the cost of equity and WACC.</li></ul><p>The last two push value down. The net effect depends on the company — you’d have to run the changes through the model to see whether the higher FCF outweighs the higher WACC.</p>", "p": 64}, {"id": "val-u2-46", "q": "How does a dividend discount model (DDM) differ from a discounted cash flow model (DCF)?", "a": "<p>The <b>DDM</b> says a company is worth the present value of all its future <b>dividends</b>; the DCF says it’s worth the present value of its future <b>free cash flows</b>.</p><p>A DDM forecasts dividends from a dividend-per-share (DPS) and growth rate assumption and discounts them at the <b>cost of equity</b>. The terminal value uses an equity multiple, usually P/E. So the DDM produces equity value (and value per share) directly — like a levered DCF, unlike an unlevered one.</p>", "p": 64}, {"id": "val-u2-47", "q": "What are the major drawbacks of the dividend discount model (DDM)?", "a": "<p>Like any forward-looking model, it’s sensitive to its inputs — payout ratio, dividend growth rate and required return. Beyond that:</p><ul><li>It can’t handle <b>high-growth</b> companies: if growth exceeds the required return, the denominator (r − g) goes negative.</li><li>It suits large, mature, consistent dividend payers — and even then, forecasting dividend growth is hard.</li><li><b>Most companies don’t pay dividends</b>, and buybacks have become a major way of returning cash — which the DDM ignores.</li><li>Dividends don’t always reflect performance: a poorly run company can still pay large dividends, distorting the valuation.</li></ul>", "p": 64}]}], "val-u3": [{"id": "val-u3-l1", "title": "Multiples & building a comps set", "pages": "65–67", "cards": [{"id": "val-u3-1", "q": "What is the purpose of using multiples in valuation?", "a": "<p>A valuation multiple expresses a company’s value relative to one metric — a value measure (equity value or EV) in the numerator over a financial or operating metric in the denominator.</p><p>Absolute values can’t be compared across companies of different sizes, so multiples standardise value on a per-unit basis. That makes similar companies comparable, which is the whole idea behind relative valuation.</p><p>A multiple only means something with context: you need to understand the company’s fundamental drivers and the industry it operates in.</p>", "p": 65}, {"id": "val-u3-2", "q": "How do you determine what the appropriate numerator is for a multiple?", "a": "<p>The numerator and denominator must represent the <b>same stakeholders</b>.</p><ul><li><b>Enterprise value</b> (all capital providers) pairs with <b>unlevered</b>, pre-debt metrics: revenue, EBITDA, EBIT, unlevered FCF.</li><li><b>Equity value</b> (shareholders only) pairs with <b>levered</b>, post-debt metrics: net income, EPS, levered FCF.</li></ul>", "p": 65}, {"id": "val-u3-3", "q": "Walk me through the process of “spreading comps.”", "a": "<p>First, clarify whether they mean trading or transaction comps — the steps overlap a lot.</p><ol><li><b>Select the peer group.</b> Trading comps: public competitors in the same or adjacent industries. Transaction comps: companies recently acquired in the same or similar industries.</li><li><b>Research.</b> Gather public information on industry trends and what drives valuations — mostly qualitative: industry developments and company specifics.</li><li><b>Pull and scrub the financials.</b> Adjust each peer for non-recurring items, accounting differences, leverage differences and business cycle effects (cyclicality, seasonality). Calendarise fiscal years if needed so the metrics line up.</li><li><b>Calculate and benchmark the multiples.</b> At minimum on an LTM and NTM basis, showing the min, max, 25th and 75th percentiles, mean and median. Use your research to explain the differences and remove outliers where justified.</li><li><b>Apply to the target.</b> Apply the median (or mean) multiple to the target’s matching metric to get an implied value.</li></ol><p>Knowing what actually drives value in the industry is what makes the result defensible — otherwise it’s hard to justify placing the target at the high or low end of the range.</p>", "p": 65}, {"id": "val-u3-4", "q": "When putting together a peer group for comps, what would some key considerations be?", "a": "<p>Start with the <b>operational profile</b>, then check the <b>financial profile</b>:</p><table><tr><th>Operational profile</th><th>Financial profile</th></tr><tr><td>Industry / sector</td><td>Size (EV, equity value)</td></tr><tr><td>Products and services</td><td>Margins (gross, operating, EBITDA)</td></tr><tr><td>End markets and customer types</td><td>Historical and forecast growth (revenue, EBITDA, EPS)</td></tr><tr><td>Distribution channels</td><td>Credit profile (leverage, coverage)</td></tr><tr><td>Cyclicality or seasonality</td><td>Returns (ROIC, ROA, ROE)</td></tr></table><p>Also consider market position (leader vs. challenger) and life-cycle stage. Choosing the right peers is the single most important part of comps.</p><p><b>Should the target be in its own peer group?</b> Many exclude it, since including it pulls the multiple towards the target’s current valuation. But if the premise of comps is that the market is right on the whole even if it misprices individual stocks, then there’s a logical case for including it.</p>", "p": 66}, {"id": "val-u3-5", "q": "What are the primary advantages of the trading comps approach?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Public filings:</b> peers are public companies, so their reports are easy to find.</li><li><b>Less data needed:</b> a proper DCF needs detailed financials; a decent comps valuation needs only a few data points (revenue, EBITDA, net income) — useful when data is limited.</li><li><b>Current:</b> it reflects today’s market prices and investor sentiment.</li></ul>", "p": 66}, {"id": "val-u3-6", "q": "What are the main disadvantages of performing trading comps?", "a": "<ul><li>Building a peer group of true “pure-play” comparables is hard, especially for a differentiated company with few direct competitors.</li><li>Even with a good peer group, it’s always somewhat “apples to oranges”, and explaining valuation gaps takes a lot of judgement.</li><li>The market can be emotional and irrational, adding outside noise.</li><li>Thinly traded or lightly followed stocks may not reflect their fundamental value, making them weaker comparables.</li></ul>", "p": 66}, {"id": "val-u3-7", "q": "To perform transaction comps, how would you compile the data?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Deal terms:</b> announcement press releases, proxy filings and the merger agreement.</li><li><b>Historical financials:</b> the target’s annual and quarterly reports.</li><li><b>Share prices and estimates:</b> research reports and data providers like Bloomberg, Capital IQ or FactSet.</li></ul>", "p": 67}, {"id": "val-u3-8", "q": "What do transaction comps tell you that trading comps cannot?", "a": "<p>They show the <b>control premiums</b> buyers and sellers should expect in a negotiation. They also confirm whether buyers actually exist in the private market, and whether a particular investment strategy has been done successfully before.</p><p>For example, a company might be valued at a certain price by a DCF, and trading comps might agree — but if there are no buyers in the market, the seller is unlikely to achieve that price in an exit.</p>", "p": 67}]}, {"id": "val-u3-l2", "title": "Control premiums, transactions & leverage", "pages": "67–69", "cards": [{"id": "val-u3-9", "q": "In M&A, why is a control premium paid?", "a": "<p>A control premium is the amount an acquirer pays above the target’s market trading price, usually expressed as a percentage.</p><p>Practically, it’s needed to persuade existing shareholders to sell — an acquirer is very unlikely to win control without offering a meaningful premium. From the target shareholders’ point of view: why would they give up their ownership if selling wasn’t worth more to them than holding?</p>", "p": 67}, {"id": "val-u3-10", "q": "Besides incentivizing existing shareholders to sell, what other factors lead to higher control premiums being paid?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Competition:</b> almost every acquisition involves a premium because sale processes are competitive; the more bidders, the higher the price goes.</li><li><b>Synergies:</b> if management is confident it can realise synergies, it can justify paying more.</li><li><b>Asset scarcity:</b> a buyer — usually a strategic — may pay up if the target is central to its plans and there’s nothing else that fits. Winning it could create a real competitive advantage.</li><li><b>Undervalued target:</b> the buyer may think the target is worth far more than its price, so a premium that looks high to others is moderate by the buyer’s own valuation.</li><li><b>Mismanagement:</b> related — an under-performing, badly run company may have lots of untapped value. The buyer expects to replace management and improve operations, restructuring right after closing.</li></ul>", "p": 67}, {"id": "val-u3-11", "q": "Why is transaction comps analysis often more challenging than trading comps?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Limited deals:</b> there may be few or no relevant transactions, or only old ones done in a very different economic environment that don’t reflect today’s market. Generally only deals from the last ~5 years are useful.</li><li><b>Context matters:</b> the form of consideration, the type of buyer (strategic vs. financial) and the deal circumstances can heavily influence the price — and need to be understood.</li><li><b>Patchy data:</b> deals involving private companies don’t have to disclose much, and sometimes even the price isn’t announced. The data is much less clean than trading comps.</li></ul>", "p": 68}, {"id": "val-u3-12", "q": "When putting together a peer group for transaction comps, what questions would you ask?", "a": "<ul><li>What was the rationale for the deal, for both the buyer and the seller?</li><li>Was the buyer a strategic or a financial buyer?</li><li>How competitive was the process — an auction or a negotiated sale?</li><li>What were economic conditions like at the time?</li><li>Was it hostile or friendly?</li><li>What was the form of consideration (cash, stock, mix)?</li><li>For a cyclical or seasonal industry, did the deal happen near a peak or a trough?</li></ul>", "p": 68}, {"id": "val-u3-13", "q": "When valuing a company using multiples, what are the trade-offs of using LTM vs. forward multiples?", "a": "<p><b>LTM (historical):</b> based on actual results, which matters because forecasts of EBITDA, EBIT and EPS are subjective — especially for smaller public companies with less reliable guidance. But historical results are often distorted by non-recurring items, which must be stripped out to get a “clean” multiple.</p><p><b>Forward:</b> companies are bought for their future potential, so forward multiples are often more relevant — but they rely on estimates.</p><p>In practice both are usually shown side by side rather than choosing one.</p>", "p": 68}, {"id": "val-u3-14", "q": "Why might two companies with identical growth and cost of capital trade at different P/E multiples?", "a": "<p>Growth and cost of capital aren’t the only value drivers — <b>return on invested capital (ROIC)</b> matters too: a company that earns more on each dollar it reinvests is worth more for the same growth.</p><p>Other reasons: different industries or geographies, plain mispricing, or EPS figures that aren’t comparable because of non-recurring items or different accounting policies.</p>", "p": 68}, {"id": "val-u3-15", "q": "Should two identical companies with different leverage ratios trade at different EV/EBITDA multiples?", "a": "<p>They should be very <b>similar</b>, because both EV and EBITDA measure value and profit independent of capital structure. They won’t be exactly equal, since EV depends on the cost of capital, which leverage affects a little.</p>", "p": 69}, {"id": "val-u3-16", "q": "Should two identical companies with different leverage ratios trade at different P/E multiples?", "a": "<p>Yes — P/E can differ a lot. More debt means more interest expense, so EPS (the denominator) falls. What happens to the share price depends on how the debt is used:</p><ul><li>If the borrowed money sits idle and earns nothing, the share price falls to reflect the extra cost with no extra growth — the P/E tends to drop.</li><li>If it’s invested to grow the business, the P/E can rise.</li></ul><p>Leverage also adds risk for equity holders, who are junior in the capital structure, which raises their required return and generally pushes P/E down.</p>", "p": 69}]}, {"id": "val-u3-l3", "title": "Common multiples & reading P/E", "pages": "69–70", "cards": [{"id": "val-u3-17", "q": "Which multiples are the most popular in valuation?", "a": "<p><b>EV/EBITDA</b> is the most common, followed by <b>EV/EBIT</b> and <b>P/E</b>. Others are industry- or company-specific:</p><ul><li><b>P/B</b> for financial institutions</li><li><b>EV/Revenue</b> for unprofitable companies</li><li><b>(EV − Capex)/EBITDA</b>-type metrics for capital-intensive industries like manufacturing or cable</li></ul>", "p": 69}, {"id": "val-u3-18", "q": "What are some common enterprise and equity value multiples?", "a": "<p><b>Enterprise value multiples:</b> EV/Revenue, EV/EBIT, EV/EBITDA.</p><p><b>Equity value multiples:</b> Price/Earnings (P/E), Price/Book (P/B), Price/Levered free cash flow.</p>", "p": 69}, {"id": "val-u3-19", "q": "Why would it be incorrect to use enterprise value and net income in a multiple?", "a": "<p>The numerator and denominator would represent different investor groups. EV is the value of the operations to <i>all</i> capital providers — lenders and shareholders. Net income is the residual that belongs only to <i>shareholders</i>, after lenders have been paid. The mismatch makes the multiple meaningless.</p>", "p": 69}, {"id": "val-u3-20", "q": "Why might one company trade at a higher multiple than another?", "a": "<p>Better fundamentals: faster expected growth, higher ROIC, a cheaper cost of capital and stronger, more reliable cash generation.</p><p>Investors look forward, so companies with better growth trajectories, good management and efficient capital allocation are rewarded with higher valuations.</p>", "p": 69}, {"id": "val-u3-21", "q": "Intuitively, what does the P/E ratio mean?", "a": "<p>P/E answers: <b>how much is the market willing to pay for one dollar of this company’s earnings?</b> It’s one of the most widely used ways to compare a company’s value with its peers and industry, to judge whether it looks under-, fairly or over-valued.</p><div class=\"formula\">P/E = Share price ÷ EPS = Market cap ÷ Net income</div>", "p": 69}, {"id": "val-u3-22", "q": "A company is currently trading at 12.5x EBITDA, is it overvalued?", "a": "<p>It depends entirely on the <b>industry</b> and how it compares with its peers. 12.5x would be around the median for a software company, but a double-digit multiple is rare in oil &amp; gas.</p><p>It’s worth knowing which industries consistently earn higher multiples (e.g. software, healthcare) and why. Differences often come from trends hitting an industry — cloud, cybersecurity, telehealth, gaming and streaming all saw big valuation jumps after the pandemic started.</p>", "p": 70}, {"id": "val-u3-23", "q": "What does a high P/E ratio relative to a peer group imply?", "a": "<p>It could mean the market is <b>overvaluing</b> the company — or simply that its current earnings are low relative to peers (for example, a younger company). That’s why P/E works best for mature companies whose peers have similar capital structures.</p><p>Most often, though, a high P/E signals <b>optimism</b>: the market expects earnings to grow strongly and believes current investment (which may be depressing today’s earnings) will pay off in higher future profits.</p>", "p": 70}, {"id": "val-u3-24", "q": "A company has an EPS of $2.00 that has declined to $1.00 four years later. Assume its share price has remained the same at $10. Is its current P/E ratio higher or lower than its four year-back P/E ratio, and how would you interpret this situation?", "a": "<p><b>Higher.</b> Four years ago: $10 ÷ $2 = 5.0x. Now: $10 ÷ $1 = 10.0x. The P/E doubled.</p><p>One reading is that it’s now overvalued given the weaker EPS — normally a fall in EPS would push the share price down. Since the price held, consider why EPS fell:</p><ul><li>The company may have <b>issued shares</b> to raise capital, diluting EPS.</li><li>It may have done a <b>stock-funded acquisition</b> that was dilutive.</li></ul><p>In both cases the drop is driven by a bigger share count (the denominator), not necessarily worse performance. The stable $10 price — which is the post-dilution price — suggests the market liked how the new capital or the deal would be used. Just as buybacks can inflate EPS, issuance can lower it without any real decline in the business.</p>", "p": 70}]}, {"id": "val-u3-l4", "title": "PEG, P/B, revenue multiples & comps technique", "pages": "70–71", "cards": [{"id": "val-u3-25", "q": "When would the use of the PEG ratio be appropriate?", "a": "<p>When comparing companies with <b>different expected growth rates</b>. PEG standardises P/E for growth.</p><div class=\"formula\">PEG = (P/E) ÷ g</div><p>Rule of thumb: PEG ≈ 1 is fairly valued, below 1 undervalued, above 1 overvalued.</p><p>The growth rate can be:</p><ul><li><b>Trailing:</b> historical growth — LTM, or annualised over the last 3–5 years.</li><li><b>Forward</b> (more common): based on 1–3 year consensus estimates, or an annualised version of them.</li></ul>", "p": 70}, {"id": "val-u3-26", "q": "When would you value a business using the P/B ratio?", "a": "<p>When book value captures a large part of what the company is really worth. Commercial banks are the classic case: most of their assets and liabilities are regularly revalued and sit close to market value.</p>", "p": 71}, {"id": "val-u3-27", "q": "When should you value a company using a revenue multiple vs. EBITDA?", "a": "<p>When the company has negative profits or EBITDA, EBITDA multiples are meaningless — so revenue multiples are the only option that gives any insight.</p>", "p": 71}, {"id": "val-u3-28", "q": "How would you value a pre-revenue early stage internet company?", "a": "<p>With <b>user and engagement metrics</b> instead of financials — subscriber count, monthly active users, daily active users, website traffic. Pre-revenue internet start-ups are often valued on multiples like EV/DAU or EV/MAU.</p>", "p": 71}, {"id": "val-u3-29", "q": "Why is calendarization a required step when performing comps analysis?", "a": "<p>Calendarisation aligns peers’ financials to the same period — by convention, adjusting everyone to a December year-end. Without it, companies with different fiscal year-ends would be compared over different time periods, and seasonality and cyclicality would distort the comparison.</p>", "p": 71}, {"id": "val-u3-30", "q": "If the market matters most when valuing public companies, do we even need a comps analysis? Why not just use the market cap directly to value the company?", "a": "<p>If markets were perfectly efficient, every stock would be correctly priced and comps would be pointless.</p><p>The reasoning for comps is that the market may be right <b>on average</b> but can misprice individual companies. Most investors agree markets are fairly efficient, but people get emotional and overreact — which creates opportunities. Comps compare a company with the market’s pricing of its peers to spot those gaps.</p>", "p": 71}, {"id": "val-u3-31", "q": "When applying a peer group derived EV/EBITDA multiple onto your target company, what is an argument for using the group’s median multiple instead of the mean?", "a": "<p>The median isn’t distorted by <b>outliers</b>. As the peer group gets bigger, the chance of outliers grows, so the median is usually the better choice. The mean can make sense for small groups (fewer than ~5 companies) with no outliers.</p>", "p": 71}]}]}};
window.IBZY_MCQS_EXTRA = (window.IBZY_MCQS_EXTRA||[]).concat([{"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l1", "d": "F", "q": "Enterprise value is best described as the value of:", "o": ["The company's operations to all capital providers", "The common equity only", "The company's cash balance", "The book value of total assets"], "a": 0, "e": "EV values the operations and belongs to all providers of capital — debt, preferred and common equity — so it is capital-structure neutral."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l1", "d": "F", "q": "Equity value = $800m, debt = $300m, cash = $100m, no preferred or minority interest. Enterprise value is:", "o": ["$600m", "$1,000m", "$1,100m", "$1,200m"], "a": 1, "e": "EV = equity value + debt − cash = 800 + 300 − 100 = $1,000m."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l1", "d": "C", "q": "The treasury stock method assumes option exercise proceeds are used to:", "o": ["Pay down debt", "Repurchase shares at the current share price", "Pay a dividend", "Fund capex"], "a": 1, "e": "TSM assumes the cash from exercising in-the-money options buys back stock at the current price, so only the net new shares are added."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l1", "d": "C", "q": "Under the treasury stock method, which options are included in the diluted share count?", "o": ["All options outstanding", "Only in-the-money options", "Only out-of-the-money options", "Only options granted this year"], "a": 1, "e": "Only in-the-money options would rationally be exercised, so only they create dilution."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l1", "d": "F", "q": "Which item is SUBTRACTED when calculating net debt?", "o": ["Preferred stock", "Bonds", "Cash and equivalents", "Term loans"], "a": 2, "e": "Net debt nets cash (and other non-operating assets) against gross debt."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l1", "d": "C", "q": "A company has more cash than debt. Compared with its equity value, its enterprise value will be:", "o": ["Higher", "Lower", "Exactly equal", "Always negative"], "a": 1, "e": "Negative net debt is subtracted, so EV falls below equity value."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l1", "d": "C", "q": "A negative enterprise value means:", "o": ["The company has negative earnings", "Net cash exceeds the company's equity value", "The company has no debt", "The share price is negative"], "a": 1, "e": "EV = equity value − net cash (when cash > debt); if net cash is larger than equity value, EV goes negative."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l1", "d": "A", "q": "Why did comparing Zoom's market cap with the airlines' market caps mislead investors?", "o": ["Market caps ignore the airlines' large debt loads and were temporarily depressed", "Zoom had more debt than the airlines", "Airlines report in a different currency", "Market cap already includes debt"], "a": 0, "e": "Equity value ignores debt-holders' claims; the heavily indebted airlines' total enterprise value was far larger than their market caps suggested."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l2", "d": "F", "q": "A company issues $250m of new debt and keeps the cash. In theory, its enterprise value:", "o": ["Rises by $250m", "Falls by $250m", "Doesn't change", "Doubles"], "a": 2, "e": "Debt and cash both rise $250m, so net debt — and EV — are unchanged."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Minority interest is added to EV because:", "o": ["It is a type of debt", "The parent consolidates 100% of the subsidiary's financials, so EV must match a 100% metric", "It reduces taxes", "It is required by IFRS only"], "a": 1, "e": "Adding minority interest keeps EV consistent with consolidated EBITDA/revenue in the denominator of multiples."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l2", "d": "C", "q": "An out-of-the-money convertible bond is treated in EV as:", "o": ["Additional diluted shares", "Debt-like financial liability", "Cash", "It is ignored completely"], "a": 1, "e": "If it's not worth converting, it behaves like debt."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l2", "d": "F", "q": "Valuing a company using multiples from recent acquisitions of similar companies is:", "o": ["Trading comps", "Transaction comps", "A DCF", "Liquidation analysis"], "a": 1, "e": "Transaction (precedent) comps use prices paid in actual M&A deals."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l2", "d": "F", "q": "Which method usually gives the highest valuation, and why?", "o": ["DCF, because of terminal value", "Trading comps, because markets are optimistic", "Transaction comps, because they include control premiums", "Liquidation, because assets are marked up"], "a": 2, "e": "Acquirers pay a control premium (often 25–50%), which inflates transaction multiples."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l2", "d": "F", "q": "Which valuation method is the most sensitive to assumptions?", "o": ["Trading comps", "Transaction comps", "DCF", "Book value"], "a": 2, "e": "The DCF depends on long-range projections, WACC and terminal value, so small input changes move the result a lot."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l2", "d": "C", "q": "A common criticism of the DCF is that:", "o": ["It ignores cash flow", "Terminal value often makes up around three-quarters of the value", "It relies only on market prices", "It can't be used for public companies"], "a": 1, "e": "Because so much value sits in the terminal value, the result leans heavily on long-run assumptions."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l2", "d": "A", "q": "Why is it misleading to say comps rely less on assumptions than a DCF?", "o": ["Comps use no data", "Comps embed implicit assumptions about cash flows, risk and growth — just other investors' assumptions", "Comps are always more accurate", "DCFs use no assumptions"], "a": 1, "e": "A multiple implicitly bakes in growth, risk and return expectations; you're simply relying on the market's assumptions instead of your own."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l3", "d": "F", "q": "The simplest definition of free cash flow is:", "o": ["Net income + D&A", "Cash from operations − capex", "EBITDA − interest", "Revenue − COGS"], "a": 1, "e": "FCF = cash from operations less capex — the cash left after the spending needed to keep operating."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l3", "d": "F", "q": "Why is capex included in FCF but acquisitions are not?", "o": ["Acquisitions are always financed with debt", "Capex is recurring and needed to operate; acquisitions are one-off and discretionary", "Acquisitions are non-cash", "Capex is tax-deductible"], "a": 1, "e": "FCF reflects recurring operations; a periodic acquisition is a one-time event."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Free cash flow yield is calculated as:", "o": ["Dividends per share ÷ share price", "FCF per share ÷ share price", "Net income ÷ equity", "FCF ÷ EBITDA"], "a": 1, "e": "FCF yield measures cash generated per share relative to the price."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Why can FCF yield be more useful than dividend yield?", "o": ["It includes debt", "It's based on cash generated, not the arbitrary amount actually paid out", "It's always higher", "It ignores capex"], "a": 1, "e": "Many companies pay no or partial dividends, so cash generated is a better fundamental measure."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l3", "d": "F", "q": "A key tax advantage of debt over equity is that:", "o": ["Dividends are tax-deductible", "Interest expense is tax-deductible", "Debt has no required payments", "Debt reduces revenue"], "a": 1, "e": "Interest is deductible (subject to limits); dividends are not."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l3", "d": "C", "q": "As companies mature, leverage ratios typically:", "o": ["Fall to zero", "Rise, because lower default risk makes debt easier and cheaper to get", "Stay constant", "Become negative"], "a": 1, "e": "A track record of profits lowers default risk, so mature firms can carry more debt."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l3", "d": "C", "q": "A $100m buyback affects the balance sheet by:", "o": ["Cash +$100m, treasury stock −$100m", "Cash −$100m, treasury stock (contra-equity) up $100m, reducing equity", "Debt +$100m", "No effect"], "a": 1, "e": "Cash falls and treasury stock rises, reducing shareholders' equity."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l3", "d": "A", "q": "Why are companies reluctant to cut dividends once started?", "o": ["Dividends are legally fixed", "Markets read a cut as a sign that profits will fall, punishing the share price", "Dividends are tax-deductible", "Cutting them raises EPS"], "a": 1, "e": "Dividends signal long-term confidence; a cut is interpreted very negatively."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l4", "d": "C", "q": "$100m net income, 15x P/E. The company borrows $200m at 5% (ignore tax) to pay a dividend, and P/E stays 15x. The net benefit to shareholders is about:", "o": ["−$150m", "$0", "$50m", "$200m"], "a": 2, "e": "Net income falls $10m to $90m, equity value falls $150m to $1,350m, but holders receive $200m: +$50m."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l4", "d": "A", "q": "In the debt-funded dividend example, the most fragile assumption is that:", "o": ["Interest is 5%", "The P/E multiple stays the same despite higher leverage", "Net income is $100m", "The dividend is paid in cash"], "a": 1, "e": "More leverage raises beta and the cost of equity, which tends to compress the P/E."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l4", "d": "F", "q": "Companies most likely to pay dividends are:", "o": ["High-growth start-ups", "Mature, low-growth companies with few projects to fund", "Companies in bankruptcy", "Companies raising equity"], "a": 1, "e": "Dividends suit companies without enough profitable reinvestment opportunities."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l4", "d": "F", "q": "An investment grows from $100 to $121 over 2 years. Its CAGR is:", "o": ["10.5%", "10%", "21%", "11%"], "a": 1, "e": "(121/100)^(1/2) − 1 = 1.1 − 1 = 10%."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l4", "d": "C", "q": "Compared with CAGR, IRR:", "o": ["Uses only beginning and ending values", "Accounts for the timing of multiple cash inflows and outflows", "Can't be calculated in Excel", "Is only for historical data"], "a": 1, "e": "IRR (XIRR) handles irregular cash flows; CAGR smooths from start to end."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l4", "d": "F", "q": "The best way to value a painting is:", "o": ["DCF", "Comparable transactions", "LBO analysis", "Book value"], "a": 1, "e": "With no cash flows, price is set by what buyers have paid for similar works."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l4", "d": "C", "q": "A sum-of-the-parts valuation is most appropriate for:", "o": ["A single-product start-up", "A conglomerate with divisions in unrelated industries", "A bank", "Any company with debt"], "a": 1, "e": "Different divisions have different risks and peers, so each is valued separately and summed."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Corporate Finance Theory", "l": "val-u1-l4", "d": "C", "q": "A private company valued using public trading comps typically gets:", "o": ["A control premium", "An illiquidity discount of roughly 10–30%", "No adjustment", "A tax gross-up"], "a": 1, "e": "Public shares carry a liquidity premium that private stakes don't have."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l1", "d": "F", "q": "In an unlevered DCF, discounting unlevered FCF at WACC gives you:", "o": ["Equity value", "Enterprise value", "Net income", "Book value"], "a": 1, "e": "Unlevered FCF belongs to all capital providers, so its present value is enterprise value."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l1", "d": "F", "q": "Unlevered free cash flow starts from:", "o": ["Net income", "EBIT, tax-effected", "Revenue", "Dividends"], "a": 1, "e": "FCFF = EBIT × (1 − t) + D&A − ΔNWC − capex; EBIT is before interest, so it's unlevered."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l1", "d": "C", "q": "The correct discount rate for levered free cash flow is:", "o": ["WACC", "Cost of equity", "Cost of debt", "Risk-free rate"], "a": 1, "e": "Levered FCF belongs only to equity holders, so it's discounted at the cost of equity."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l1", "d": "C", "q": "In the WACC formula, the cost of debt is multiplied by (1 − t) because:", "o": ["Debt is riskier", "Interest is tax-deductible", "Equity is taxed", "It converts book to market value"], "a": 1, "e": "The interest tax shield lowers the effective cost of debt."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l1", "d": "F", "q": "WACC weights should be based on:", "o": ["Book values", "Market values", "Par values", "Historical cost"], "a": 1, "e": "WACC uses the market values of debt and equity."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l1", "d": "F", "q": "Rf = 4%, beta = 1.5, equity risk premium = 6%. The cost of equity under CAPM is:", "o": ["10%", "13%", "9%", "15%"], "a": 1, "e": "4% + 1.5 × 6% = 13%."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l1", "d": "F", "q": "For US companies, the usual proxy for the risk-free rate is:", "o": ["3-month T-bill", "10-year US Treasury yield", "Fed funds rate", "S&P 500 return"], "a": 1, "e": "Long-dated bonds are illiquid, so the 10-year Treasury is used."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l1", "d": "C", "q": "All else equal, falling interest rates make DCF valuations:", "o": ["Lower", "Higher", "Unchanged", "Negative"], "a": 1, "e": "A lower risk-free rate lowers the discount rate, raising present values."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l1", "d": "C", "q": "A company with no debt has a WACC equal to:", "o": ["Its cost of debt", "Its cost of equity", "Zero", "The risk-free rate"], "a": 1, "e": "With a 0% debt weight, WACC is just the cost of equity."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l2", "d": "F", "q": "The equity risk premium is:", "o": ["Expected market return − risk-free rate", "Cost of debt − risk-free rate", "Beta × market return", "Dividend yield + growth"], "a": 0, "e": "ERP is the excess return of equities over risk-free assets, historically about 4–6%."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l2", "d": "F", "q": "A stock with a beta of 2.0 would be expected to rise by about how much if the market rises 10%?", "o": ["5%", "10%", "20%", "2%"], "a": 2, "e": "Beta of 2 implies twice the market's move."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l2", "d": "F", "q": "Which risk can be eliminated through diversification?", "o": ["Systematic risk", "Market risk", "Unsystematic (company-specific) risk", "Interest-rate risk"], "a": 2, "e": "Company- and industry-specific risk diversifies away; market risk doesn't."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l2", "d": "C", "q": "All else equal, a higher beta leads to:", "o": ["A higher valuation", "A lower valuation", "No change", "A lower cost of equity"], "a": 1, "e": "Higher beta → higher cost of equity and discount rate → lower present value."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l2", "d": "F", "q": "Which sector would typically have the LOWEST beta?", "o": ["Semiconductors", "Automobiles", "Utilities", "Construction"], "a": 2, "e": "Utilities provide essentials needed in any economy, so they're less sensitive to the market."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Why are peer betas unlevered before being applied to a target?", "o": ["To remove the effect of their different capital structures", "To increase the beta", "Because private companies have no debt", "To convert to book value"], "a": 0, "e": "Unlevering isolates business risk; you then relever at the target's own leverage."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Levered beta 1.2, D/E 0.5, tax rate 20%. Unlevered beta is about:", "o": ["0.86", "1.2", "1.68", "0.6"], "a": 0, "e": "1.2 ÷ [1 + 0.8 × 0.5] = 1.2 ÷ 1.4 ≈ 0.86."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l2", "d": "A", "q": "Which statement about levered and unlevered beta is correct?", "o": ["Levered beta is always lower", "Levered beta is equal to or greater than unlevered beta", "They're unrelated", "Unlevered beta includes financial risk"], "a": 1, "e": "Debt adds financial risk on top of business risk, so levered beta ≥ unlevered beta."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l2", "d": "C", "q": "An example of an asset that can have a negative beta is:", "o": ["A tech stock", "Gold", "A utility", "An airline"], "a": 1, "e": "Gold often rises when equities fall, as investors seek a safe haven."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l3", "d": "C", "q": "For a company without traded debt, the cost of debt can be estimated using:", "o": ["Its dividend yield", "A synthetic rating based on interest coverage", "Its P/E ratio", "The equity risk premium"], "a": 1, "e": "Infer a rating from coverage, then add that rating's default spread to the risk-free rate."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l3", "d": "F", "q": "Why is the cost of equity usually higher than the cost of debt?", "o": ["Equity is senior to debt", "Equity holders have no guaranteed payments and are last in liquidation; interest is also tax-deductible", "Equity has no risk", "Dividends are tax-deductible"], "a": 1, "e": "Equity is riskier and debt benefits from the interest tax shield."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l3", "d": "A", "q": "As a company adds debt beyond its optimal capital structure, WACC:", "o": ["Keeps falling", "Starts rising as distress costs outweigh the tax shield", "Stays flat", "Becomes equal to the risk-free rate"], "a": 1, "e": "WACC falls with moderate leverage, then rises once financial distress risk dominates."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l3", "d": "F", "q": "A project should generally be pursued when:", "o": ["IRR < WACC", "IRR > WACC", "WACC = 0", "IRR is negative"], "a": 1, "e": "If the return exceeds the cost of capital, the project creates value."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Which input usually moves a DCF valuation the most?", "o": ["Sales growth rate", "Discount rate", "Tax rate on year 1", "Number of shares"], "a": 1, "e": "The discount rate affects every cash flow and the terminal value."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l3", "d": "F", "q": "Final-year FCF next year = $100m, r = 10%, g = 2%. Terminal value is:", "o": ["$1,000m", "$1,250m", "$833m", "$5,000m"], "a": 1, "e": "TV = 100 ÷ (0.10 − 0.02) = $1,250m."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l3", "d": "C", "q": "A reasonable long-term perpetuity growth rate is usually:", "o": ["8–10%", "1–3%", "Equal to WACC", "Above the discount rate"], "a": 1, "e": "It must be sustainable forever, so it's anchored to GDP or the risk-free rate."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Why is the terminal value discounted back to today?", "o": ["It's already in today's dollars", "It's a value as of the end of the forecast period, so it's a future value", "To add the tax shield", "Because it's always negative"], "a": 1, "e": "TV is measured at the end of the explicit period and must be brought to present value."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l3", "d": "A", "q": "The main conceptual criticism of the exit multiple approach is that it:", "o": ["Ignores EBITDA", "Introduces market-based relative valuation into an intrinsic method", "Can't be sanity-checked", "Always gives a lower value"], "a": 1, "e": "It makes the DCF partly depend on how the market prices comparables."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l4", "d": "C", "q": "Using a mid-year convention, a Year 5 cash flow is discounted using:", "o": ["5 years", "4.5 years", "5.5 years", "1 year"], "a": 1, "e": "Cash is assumed to arrive mid-period, so half a year earlier."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l4", "d": "F", "q": "Switching from end-of-year to mid-year convention makes the valuation:", "o": ["Lower", "Higher", "Unchanged", "Negative"], "a": 1, "e": "Cash flows are assumed to arrive earlier, so they're discounted less."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l4", "d": "C", "q": "The mid-year convention is least appropriate for:", "o": ["A utility", "A software subscription business", "A highly seasonal winter clothing retailer", "A bank"], "a": 2, "e": "If most cash arrives late in the year, mid-year overstates timing."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l4", "d": "C", "q": "Two firms have the same Debt/EBITDA but one holds far more cash. Which has lower default risk?", "o": ["The one with less cash", "The one with more cash", "They're identical", "Can't be compared"], "a": 1, "e": "Cash can repay debt, so the cash-rich firm has lower Net Debt/EBITDA and lower risk."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l4", "d": "F", "q": "A DCF is least credible when:", "o": ["The company is mature and stable", "The company won't generate positive cash flow for many years", "The company has publicly traded debt", "WACC is known"], "a": 1, "e": "Value becomes concentrated in distant, highly uncertain cash flows."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l4", "d": "A", "q": "If 80% of a DCF value comes from the terminal value, a sensible step is to:", "o": ["Ignore it", "Check whether the forecast period is long enough and the terminal assumptions are reasonable", "Raise the growth rate", "Remove the terminal value"], "a": 1, "e": "A short forecast or aggressive TV assumptions often cause excessive TV weight."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l4", "d": "C", "q": "Raising significant new debt in an unlevered DCF mainly affects value through:", "o": ["Free cash flow", "Changes in capital structure weights and the cost of debt within WACC", "Revenue", "The share count"], "a": 1, "e": "Unlevered FCF is unchanged; WACC can change if leverage shifts materially."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l4", "d": "C", "q": "Standard practice includes which options in the diluted share count?", "o": ["All options", "Vested, in-the-money options (via TSM)", "Out-of-the-money options", "Only options granted to the CEO"], "a": 1, "e": "Exercisable in-the-money options, with proceeds assumed to repurchase shares."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l5", "d": "C", "q": "Share price $60. Convertible preferred: 10m shares, $50 liquidation value each, convertible 1:1. You should:", "o": ["Treat it as debt", "Assume conversion, add 10m shares and remove the preferred from net debt", "Ignore it", "Add it to both shares and net debt"], "a": 1, "e": "Converting is worth more ($60 > $50), so assume conversion and avoid double counting."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l5", "d": "A", "q": "If a convertible bond is assumed to convert, in net debt you should:", "o": ["Keep it as debt", "Remove it to avoid double counting", "Double it", "Add accrued interest"], "a": 1, "e": "It's already in the share count, so it shouldn't also sit in net debt."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l5", "d": "C", "q": "When leases are significant, a DCF should:", "o": ["Ignore lease obligations", "Remove rent from FCF and include the PV of lease obligations in net debt", "Double-count rent", "Add leases to revenue"], "a": 1, "e": "Leases behave like debt, so they're capitalised into net debt."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l5", "d": "C", "q": "In the terminal period of a DCF, the tax rate should be close to:", "o": ["Zero", "The marginal tax rate", "The lowest historical effective rate", "The dividend payout ratio"], "a": 1, "e": "Deferrals unwind over time, so the perpetuity rate should be normalised towards the marginal rate."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l5", "d": "A", "q": "Lowering the tax rate in a DCF:", "o": ["Always raises value", "Raises FCF but also raises the after-tax cost of debt and levered beta, so the net effect varies", "Always lowers value", "Has no effect"], "a": 1, "e": "Higher cash flow vs. a higher WACC — you need to run it through the model."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l5", "d": "F", "q": "A dividend discount model discounts dividends at the:", "o": ["WACC", "Cost of equity", "Cost of debt", "Risk-free rate"], "a": 1, "e": "Dividends go only to equity holders, so the cost of equity is used."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l5", "d": "C", "q": "Why can't the DDM's growth formula be used for very high-growth companies?", "o": ["They have no equity", "If g exceeds r, the denominator (r − g) turns negative", "They're too large", "Dividends aren't taxed"], "a": 1, "e": "The perpetuity formula breaks when growth exceeds the required return."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Intrinsic Valuation", "l": "val-u2-l5", "d": "C", "q": "A key limitation of the DDM today is that it:", "o": ["Includes buybacks twice", "Ignores buybacks, even though many companies return cash that way", "Uses WACC", "Requires EBITDA"], "a": 1, "e": "Most companies pay little or no dividend and buybacks are a big source of shareholder return."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l1", "d": "F", "q": "Which pairing is consistent?", "o": ["EV / net income", "Equity value / EBITDA", "EV / EBITDA", "Equity value / revenue"], "a": 2, "e": "EV (all capital providers) pairs with unlevered metrics like EBITDA."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l1", "d": "F", "q": "P/E pairs equity value with net income because both are:", "o": ["Unlevered", "Levered — they belong to equity holders after debt", "Pre-tax", "Cash-based"], "a": 1, "e": "Both measure value/earnings attributable to shareholders after debt costs."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l1", "d": "C", "q": "'Scrubbing' comps financials means:", "o": ["Deleting outliers only", "Adjusting for non-recurring items, accounting differences and other distortions so peers are comparable", "Converting to a different currency", "Using book values"], "a": 1, "e": "Normalising lets you compare core operating performance fairly."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l1", "d": "C", "q": "Comps output usually shows which statistics for the peer multiples?", "o": ["Only the mean", "Min, max, 25th/75th percentile, mean and median", "Only the target's multiple", "Standard deviation only"], "a": 1, "e": "A full range gives context for where the target should sit."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l1", "d": "F", "q": "Which is part of a company's operational profile when choosing peers?", "o": ["EBITDA margin", "End markets and customers", "Debt/EBITDA", "ROIC"], "a": 1, "e": "Business model, products, end markets and cyclicality make up the operational profile."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l1", "d": "F", "q": "A key advantage of trading comps is that it:", "o": ["Needs detailed forecasts", "Reflects current market pricing and needs relatively little data", "Always gives the highest value", "Ignores market sentiment"], "a": 1, "e": "Only a few metrics are needed, and prices are up to date."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l1", "d": "C", "q": "A weakness of trading comps is that:", "o": ["Data is hard to find", "True pure-play comparables are rare and markets can be irrational", "It ignores market prices", "It requires a WACC"], "a": 1, "e": "Comparisons are rarely like-for-like and prices can be noisy."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l1", "d": "C", "q": "What can transaction comps reveal that trading comps can't?", "o": ["The risk-free rate", "Typical control premiums and whether buyers exist for such assets", "Beta", "Future dividends"], "a": 1, "e": "They show what acquirers actually paid, including premiums."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l2", "d": "F", "q": "A control premium is:", "o": ["The fee paid to bankers", "The amount paid above the target's market price to gain control", "A discount for illiquidity", "The target's book value"], "a": 1, "e": "It compensates shareholders for giving up control/ownership."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Which factor would most likely INCREASE the control premium?", "o": ["Only one bidder", "Several competing bidders", "No synergies", "A fairly valued, well-run target"], "a": 1, "e": "Competition drives the price up."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l2", "d": "C", "q": "A buyer pays a big premium for a badly run company because:", "o": ["It wants to keep management", "It expects to unlock value by replacing management and fixing operations", "Badly run companies are always cheap", "The law requires it"], "a": 1, "e": "Mismanagement means untapped value the buyer believes it can capture."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Why are transaction comps often harder than trading comps?", "o": ["Too many recent deals", "Few relevant recent deals and patchy disclosure, especially for private targets", "Public data is too detailed", "They ignore premiums"], "a": 1, "e": "Deal data can be scarce, dated, and context-dependent."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l2", "d": "F", "q": "Generally, transaction comps should be limited to deals from roughly the last:", "o": ["1 year", "5 years", "20 years", "50 years"], "a": 1, "e": "Older deals reflect different market conditions."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l2", "d": "C", "q": "A drawback of LTM multiples is that:", "o": ["They're based on estimates", "Historical results can include non-recurring items that distort the multiple", "They're never available", "They include control premiums"], "a": 1, "e": "One-offs must be scrubbed to get a clean multiple."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Two companies with the same growth and cost of capital trade at different P/Es. A likely explanation is:", "o": ["Different ROIC", "Same industry", "Identical accounting", "Same share count"], "a": 0, "e": "Higher returns on invested capital justify a higher multiple."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l2", "d": "A", "q": "Two identical companies differ only in leverage. Which multiple should be MOST similar?", "o": ["P/E", "EV/EBITDA", "Dividend yield", "P/B"], "a": 1, "e": "EV/EBITDA is largely capital-structure neutral; P/E is affected by interest expense."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l3", "d": "F", "q": "The most commonly used valuation multiple is:", "o": ["P/B", "EV/EBITDA", "Dividend yield", "EV/Subscribers"], "a": 1, "e": "EV/EBITDA is the workhorse multiple, followed by EV/EBIT and P/E."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l3", "d": "F", "q": "Which is an EQUITY value multiple?", "o": ["EV/Revenue", "EV/EBIT", "P/B", "EV/EBITDA"], "a": 2, "e": "P/B uses market cap (price) over book equity."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Why is EV/Net income an invalid multiple?", "o": ["Net income is too volatile", "EV represents all capital providers while net income belongs only to equity holders", "Net income is pre-tax", "EV is book value"], "a": 1, "e": "The numerator and denominator represent different stakeholder groups."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l3", "d": "F", "q": "Which typically justifies a HIGHER multiple?", "o": ["Lower growth", "Higher WACC", "Higher ROIC and stronger growth", "Weaker cash flow"], "a": 2, "e": "Better fundamentals earn higher valuations."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l3", "d": "F", "q": "Market cap $500m, net income $25m. P/E is:", "o": ["5x", "20x", "25x", "12.5x"], "a": 1, "e": "500 ÷ 25 = 20x."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Is a company at 12.5x EV/EBITDA overvalued?", "o": ["Always yes", "Always no", "It depends on its industry and peer multiples", "Only if it has debt"], "a": 2, "e": "Multiples must be judged against the sector and peers."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l3", "d": "C", "q": "EPS falls from $2 to $1 while the price stays at $10. The P/E:", "o": ["Halves to 2.5x", "Stays at 5x", "Doubles from 5x to 10x", "Falls to 1x"], "a": 2, "e": "$10/$2 = 5x before; $10/$1 = 10x now."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l3", "d": "A", "q": "In that example, a plausible reason EPS fell without the price falling is:", "o": ["A share buyback", "Share issuance or a stock-funded deal increased the share count, and the market liked the use of funds", "Lower taxes", "Higher revenue"], "a": 1, "e": "Dilution can cut EPS without a real decline in the business."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l4", "d": "F", "q": "P/E of 20x and expected growth of 20%. The PEG ratio is:", "o": ["0.5", "1.0", "4.0", "20"], "a": 1, "e": "PEG = 20 ÷ 20 = 1.0 — roughly fair value by the rule of thumb."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l4", "d": "C", "q": "By the PEG rule of thumb, a PEG of 0.6 suggests the stock is:", "o": ["Overvalued", "Undervalued", "Fairly valued", "Bankrupt"], "a": 1, "e": "Below 1 implies the P/E is low relative to growth."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l4", "d": "F", "q": "P/B is most commonly used to value:", "o": ["Software companies", "Commercial banks", "Start-ups", "Oil producers"], "a": 1, "e": "Bank assets and liabilities are frequently marked close to market value."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l4", "d": "F", "q": "An unprofitable company with negative EBITDA is best valued with:", "o": ["EV/EBITDA", "P/E", "EV/Revenue", "PEG"], "a": 2, "e": "Negative earnings make earnings multiples meaningless."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l4", "d": "C", "q": "A pre-revenue social app could be valued using:", "o": ["P/E", "EV/MAU or EV/DAU", "Dividend discount model", "P/B"], "a": 1, "e": "User engagement metrics stand in for financials."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l4", "d": "C", "q": "Calendarisation in comps means:", "o": ["Removing seasonal businesses", "Aligning peers' financials to a common fiscal period", "Converting currencies", "Using only Q4 numbers"], "a": 1, "e": "It ensures all peers are measured over the same time period."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l4", "d": "C", "q": "Why use the median peer multiple rather than the mean?", "o": ["It's always higher", "It's less distorted by outliers", "It's required by GAAP", "It includes the target"], "a": 1, "e": "Outliers can drag a mean far from the typical company."}, {"t": "Valuation", "u": "Valuation · Relative Valuation", "l": "val-u3-l4", "d": "A", "q": "The rationale for comps even though public prices exist is that:", "o": ["Markets are always wrong", "Markets may be right on average but misprice individual companies", "Market caps are unavailable", "Comps ignore market prices"], "a": 1, "e": "Comparing to peers can reveal individual mispricings."}]);
